    {
      "_type": "UMLActivity",
      "_id": "37f2dc037d2d418b90d2d0626449117c",
      "_parent": {
        "$ref": "56c38c347ec045c3b6adbe367eccbed4"
      },
      "name": "AD-09 - Medicine Catalogue Management",
      "visibility": "public",
      "isAbstract": false,
      "isReadOnly": false,
      "isSingleExecution": false,
      "documentation": "Activity Diagram derived from UC-19 (Manage Medicine Catalogue), realizing MedAccess_AD09_Medicine_Catalogue_Management.md.\n\nShows the workflow through which an Administrator opens catalogue management, creates or selects a medicine record, enters/updates structured medicine information (name, active ingredient, strength, dosage form, pack size, manufacturer where applicable), and the system validates the data, checks for duplicate/inconsistent records, stores the change, and records the administrative action for audit.\n\nPreconditions:\n1. The Administrator can access catalogue management.\n2. The Administrator is authenticated (authentication itself is out of scope of this diagram).\n\nPostconditions (success): The catalogue change is stored and the administrative action is recorded in the audit trail.\nPostconditions (alternative): Access is denied for an unauthorized administrator, OR invalid catalogue data is rejected and returned for correction, OR a duplicate/inconsistent record is flagged and returned for correction/review.\n\nScope boundary: Catalogue management is not clinical recommendation and does not imply therapeutic equivalence between records with the same active ingredient. No diagnosis, prescribing, treatment recommendation, automatic medicine equivalence, automatic substitution, or database/API implementation is modeled here.\n\nNote: 'Continue Catalogue Management?' is modeled as an optional repeat loop per the source document's compact flow and quality checklist, allowing the Administrator to return to the catalogue-records view for further changes instead of ending the activity after a single change.\n\nTraceability: AD-09 -> UC-19 - Manage Medicine Catalogue; FR-05-FR-09, FR-35-FR-39.",
      "ownedElements": [
